This is a beta-test release of blarslib, a unix-compatability libraryfor os9/68k 2.3.  It is not well documented, but manuals from both BSDand System V unix will help.  In cases where BSD and System V haddifferent names for the same function, both may be supplied.  If youwould like to help document, or have other sugestions, let me know.Some of these functions have been distributed before.  I releasedvarargs.h, link/rename, and the v*printf family seperatly.  Alloca isfrom Doug Gwen.  Many of the string functions are from Henry Spencer.All may be distributed for free.The Makefile will need to be customized for each system, if nothingelse just change the hostname.  It does assume /r0 may be used fortemporary files.My Makefiles and the following assume blarslib has been installed in/h0/blarslib .  The Defs directory cannot and should not be mergedwith /dd/defs, since it supplies replacements which require the/dd/defs version as well.cc should be used with the -v=/h0/blarslib/defs option to force it tolook there for <insert> files before /dd/defs .l68 should use the -l=/h0/blarslib/blarslib.l option to search theblarslib library for needed functions.Other portability problems to watch for:'\n' is ascii CR not LF as in unix.  Most programs this breaks use '\r'in conjunction with '\n', so searching for \r may help locate this problem.The arguments to open(), creat(), and mkdir() differ from unix.Constants are defined in <fileinfo.h> which may be used in both OSKand unix.Standard devices and directories differ.  NULLDEV (defined in<fileinfo.h>) is "/dev/null" for unix and "/nil" for osk.  Unixprograms may assume the existance of /tmp, /usr/tmp, /usr/lib, andother directories.Terminal handling is very different.  The unix functions cannot beemulated close enough to be useful in most instances.Unix fork() cannot be emulated.  In most cases, the section of codeusing fork() can be rewritten to use os9fork(), but this is non-trivial.